      "problem": "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
