      "dom_id": "question-231755",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $4$th term is ${a}_{4}=-135,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{4-1} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{3} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-27) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5(-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
