      "name": null,
      "problem": "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$",
